Many people report seeing this behavior when using the SEC API specifically which makes me think that service is requesting this.  Maybe email is required by the SEC API?<p>---<p>UPDATE: The SEC API documentation requires your email in the User-Agent and will reject you otherwise. Demo:<p><pre><code>    U=&#x27;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;data.sec.gov&#x2F;api&#x2F;xbrl&#x2F;companyconcept&#x2F;CIK0000320193&#x2F;us-gaap&#x2F;Assets.json&#x27;
    curl -s -o &#x2F;dev&#x2F;null -w &#x27;response: %{http_code}  curl default User-Agent, rejected\n&#x27;  &quot;$U&quot;
    curl -s -o &#x2F;dev&#x2F;null -w &#x27;response: %{http_code}  email in user-agent, success\n&#x27; -A &#x27;Acme Research admin@acme-research.example&#x27; &quot;$U&quot;

    response: 403  curl default, rejected
    response: 200  email in user-agent, success



</code></pre>
SEC docs: <a href="https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.sec.gov&#x2F;search-filings&#x2F;edgar-search-assistance&#x2F;accessing-edgar-data#:~:text=Bot%20Request%20Headers%3A-,User%2DAgent%3A,-Sample%20Company%20Name" rel="nofollow">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.sec.gov&#x2F;search-filings&#x2F;edgar-search-assistance&#x2F;a...</a><p>So basically Claude is trying to problem solve, and just does what the SEC asks. It&#x27;s a &#x27;prompt injection attack&#x27;, but the entity instructing this behavior is the SEC. Tricky alignment problem. Pretty silly mechanism, since of course there&#x27;s no email validation.  (edit: turns out the SEC will accept most any non-cURL user-agent, email not actually required)
